{
  "comprehensible": "The committee had believed that it was a routine oversight — an ordinary case of someone failing to notice something by accident. In fact, it was a deliberate omission: the thing had been left out on purpose, so that the minister would not have to face a difficult question.",
  "glosses": [
    {
      "expression": "had taken for",
      "gloss": "had believed to be; had understood to be (even though that was wrong)"
    },
    {
      "expression": "routine oversight",
      "gloss": "an ordinary mistake in which someone fails to notice or check something; here 'oversight' does not mean 'supervision'"
    },
    {
      "expression": "in fact",
      "gloss": "used to contrast the truth with what was believed before"
    },
    {
      "expression": "a deliberate omission",
      "gloss": "something left out on purpose, not by accident"
    },
    {
      "expression": "designed to",
      "gloss": "intended to; planned for the purpose of"
    },
    {
      "expression": "to spare the minister an awkward question",
      "gloss": "to save the minister from having to deal with a question that would be embarrassing or hard to answer"
    }
  ],
  "grammar": "The subject of the main verb 'was' is not a noun but a whole clause: 'What the committee had taken for a routine oversight.' Here 'What' means 'the thing that', so the sentence reads: 'The thing that the committee had taken for a routine oversight was in fact a deliberate omission.' English often builds sentences this way to put a long description first and then reveal what it really was. Also note that 'designed to spare...' is a participle phrase attached to 'omission', not to the committee: it gives the purpose of the omission.",
  "translation": "委员会原以为这只是一次普通的疏忽，事实上那是有意省略的，目的是让部长不必面对一个难堪的问题。"
}
